;; Docs for std/inbox.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "inbox.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "A bounded queue that .NET threads put items into and fibers take items out of.")
  (see make-inbox inbox-post! inbox-recv inbox-closed inbox-call)
  (example """
    (import (std inbox))

    (: changes (Inbox string))
    (def changes (make-inbox 1000 DropOldest))

    ;; The callback that .NET calls on its own thread. It posts, and returns.
    (: on-change (-> string void))
    (defun (on-change path)
      (ignore (inbox-post! changes path)))

    ;; The fiber takes the items, with its own handlers and parameters.
    (: watch (-bjo-> (Inbox string) int))
    (defbjo (watch ib)
      (let go ((n 0))
        (match (sync (choose (wrap (inbox-recv ib) Some)
                             (wrap (inbox-closed ib) (fun (_) None))))
          ((Some path) (log (str "changed: " path)) (go (+ n 1)))
          (None n))))
    """)
  (example """
    ;; Request and reply: a .NET caller waits on a task, a fiber answers.
    (: requests (Inbox (Call int int)))
    (def requests (make-inbox 64 DropNewest))

    ;; On the .NET thread: (inbox-call requests 21) gives a (Task int).

    ;; On the fiber:
    (: serve (-bjo-> (Inbox (Call int int)) void))
    (defbjo (serve ib)
      (let ((c (sync (inbox-recv ib))))
        (try (ignore (call-reply! c (* 2 (call-request c))))
             ;; Does nothing when the reply above won.
             #:finally (ignore (call-fail! c (Exception. "no answer"))))
        (serve ib)))
    """)
  (reference """
    An inbox is the door that .NET code uses to give work to Bjolang. A
    thread that is not a fiber, for example a Kestrel thread, a timer thread
    or the thread of a @code{FileSystemWatcher}, gives an item to an inbox
    and returns. A fiber takes the item. The fiber has its own dynamic
    environment: its effect handlers, its parameters and its cancellation
    scope.

    @strong{The rule for a lambda that .NET calls.} It may call the functions
    in this module and nothing else. The functions that a foreign thread uses
    are ordinary functions, not bjoroutines, and they do not read parameters
    or effect handlers, because a thread that is not a fiber has none. A
    @code{(log ...)} in such a lambda does not fail. It goes to the default
    handler, also inside a @code{with-handler}. So post the work, and do the
    logging in the fiber.

    @strong{Capacity.} Every inbox has a capacity, and there is no default.
    An event source that is faster than its consumer must not grow memory
    without limit. @code{make-inbox} takes a @code{WhenFull} policy that
    tells what @code{inbox-post!} and @code{inbox-call} do when the inbox is
    full. @code{inbox-send} and @code{inbox-send-evt} wait for room and
    never drop an item.

    @strong{Events.} @code{inbox-recv}, @code{inbox-send-evt} and
    @code{inbox-closed} are CML events. A receive or a send that loses its
    @code{choose} takes nothing and puts nothing. Items come out in the
    order they went in. Senders that wait for room get in first in, first
    out, for the task form and the event form together.

    @strong{Waking.} A post from a thread that is not a fiber does not run
    the receiving fiber on that thread. That thread can hold a lock of a
    .NET library, and the fiber could call back into the library and
    deadlock. So the receiver is queued on the scheduler, at the cost of one
    thread-pool hop. A post from a fiber wakes the receiver directly.

    @strong{The end.} @code{inbox-close!} and @code{inbox-fail!} are a
    graceful end: what is queued can still be received. @code{inbox-abort!}
    is a hard stop: it drops what is queued. @code{inbox-closed} fires when
    the inbox is closed and nothing is left to receive.

    @strong{Request and reply.} An inbox whose items are @code{Call}s is a
    request/reply inbox. Capacity, the policy, closing and receiving work as
    they do for any other inbox. A fiber that takes a @code{Call} must answer
    it, usually in @code{#:finally}, so that a raise also answers it.
    Otherwise the .NET caller waits forever.

    @code{BjolangRuntime/Cml/Inbox.cs} has the implementation.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Making one, and the policy it needs
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc WhenFull
  (summary "What a full inbox does with an item from inbox-post! or inbox-call.")
  (case DropNewest "Refuse the new item. @code{inbox-post!} returns @code{#f}, and the task of a refused call faults.")
  (case DropOldest "Remove the oldest queued item and queue the new item. The task of a removed call faults.")
  (see make-inbox inbox-dropped-count)
  (reference """
    The policy has no effect on @code{inbox-send}, @code{inbox-send/token}
    or @code{inbox-send-evt}. They wait for room and never drop an item.

    A faulted task holds a @code{Bjoml.InboxFullException}. Both policies
    add each item that they drop to @code{inbox-dropped-count}.
    """))

(:doc make-inbox
  (summary "A new, empty inbox that holds at most capacity queued items.")
  (arg capacity "The most items that can wait in the queue. At least 1.")
  (arg policy "What @code{inbox-post!} and @code{inbox-call} do when the inbox is full.")
  (returns "A new, open inbox.")
  (raises System.ArgumentOutOfRangeException "When capacity is less than 1.")
  (see WhenFull inbox-post! inbox-recv)
  (example """
    (def events (make-inbox 1000 DropOldest))
    """)
  (reference """
    There is no unbounded inbox and no default capacity. An event source
    that is faster than its consumer must not grow memory without limit: a
    @code{FileSystemWatcher} fires thousands of events during a large
    @code{git checkout}. For an HTTP server, the capacity is the number of
    requests that can wait before the answer is 503.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Putting something in
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc inbox-post!
  (summary "Gives x to the inbox and never waits. Safe from any thread.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (arg x "The item.")
  (returns "@code{#t} when x was queued or given to a waiting receiver. @code{#f} when the inbox is closed, or full under @code{DropNewest}.")
  (see inbox-send inbox-send-evt WhenFull inbox-dropped-count)
  (reference """
    This is the function for a thread that has no fiber context. When the
    inbox is full, the @code{WhenFull} policy decides. Under
    @code{DropOldest} the oldest queued item is removed, x is queued, and
    the answer is @code{#t}.

    If the item is a @code{Call} and it is refused or removed, its task
    faults with a @code{Bjoml.InboxFullException}.

    Under @code{DropOldest} the answer can also be @code{#f} for a short
    time when every place in the queue is promised to a sender that is
    committing at that moment, because then there is no queued item to
    remove.
    """))

(:doc inbox-send
  (summary "Gives x to the inbox, as a task that completes when x is in. Safe from any thread.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (arg x "The item.")
  (returns "A task that completes when x is queued or given to a receiver.")
  (see inbox-send/token inbox-send-evt inbox-post!)
  (reference """
    When the inbox is full, the task stays pending until a receiver makes
    room. This is backpressure: each producer that waits holds exactly one
    item. The @code{WhenFull} policy has no effect here; nothing is
    dropped.

    On a closed inbox, the task is cancelled at once. If
    @code{inbox-abort!} comes while the send waits, the task is cancelled
    and x is not queued. After @code{inbox-close!}, a send that already
    waits still gets in.

    A fiber uses @code{inbox-send-evt} instead.
    """))

(:doc inbox-send/token
  (summary "The same as inbox-send, with a cancellation token of the caller.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (arg x "The item.")
  (arg token "The caller's token, for example Kestrel's @code{RequestAborted}.")
  (returns "A task that completes when x is queued or given to a receiver.")
  (see inbox-send inbox-call/token)
  (reference """
    If the token fires while the send waits for room, x does not go into
    the queue and the task is cancelled. When there is room at once, x goes
    in and the token has no effect.
    """))

(:doc inbox-send-evt
  (summary "The event that puts x in the inbox, for a fiber.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (arg x "The item.")
  (returns "An event that commits when x goes in.")
  (see inbox-send inbox-closed)
  (example """
    (sync (choose (inbox-send-evt ib job)
                  (wrap (inbox-closed ib) (fun (_) (log "nobody takes jobs now")))))
    """)
  (reference """
    The event commits when there is room. If another branch of a
    @code{choose} wins, x is @strong{not} queued.

    That is why a fiber gets an event and not a task. A task cannot be
    withdrawn, so @code{(task->event (inbox-send ib x))} in a branch that
    loses queues x later anyway, and nobody expects it.

    On a closed inbox, the event never commits. After @code{inbox-abort!},
    a send event that waits never commits. A fiber that can meet a closed
    inbox chooses this event together with @code{inbox-closed}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Taking it out, and the end of the stream
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc inbox-recv
  (summary "The event that takes the oldest item from the inbox.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (returns "An event whose value is the item.")
  (see inbox-closed inbox-post!)
  (reference """
    This is a real CML event. If another branch of a @code{choose} wins,
    nothing is taken, and the item stays at the front for the next
    receiver, as with a channel receive.

    On an inbox that is closed and empty, it never fires. Use
    @code{inbox-closed} to see the end.
    """))

(:doc inbox-closed
  (summary "The event that fires when the inbox is closed and nothing is left to receive.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (returns "An event whose value is @code{None} after @code{inbox-close!} or @code{inbox-abort!}, and @code{(Some e)} after @code{(inbox-fail! ib e)}.")
  (see inbox-recv inbox-close! inbox-fail! inbox-abort!)
  (example """
    (defbjo (drain ib)
      (let go ()
        (match (sync (choose (wrap (inbox-recv ib) Some)
                             (wrap (inbox-closed ib) (fun (_) None))))
          ((Some x) (handle x) (go))
          (None 'done))))
    """)
  (reference """
    It fires when the inbox is closed, the queue is empty, and no sender
    that waited before the close is still to get in. After that, every
    @code{sync} on it commits at once.

    It is a separate event, and not an @code{Option} from
    @code{inbox-recv}, so that code which never closes its inbox keeps the
    simple form. An example is an HTTP worker that stops when its scope is
    cancelled.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Ending it
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc inbox-close!
  (summary "Closes the inbox gracefully: what is queued can still be received.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (see inbox-fail! inbox-abort! inbox-closed)
  (reference """
    After the close, @code{inbox-post!} returns @code{#f}, and new sends and
    calls get a cancelled task. A new @code{inbox-send-evt} never commits.

    What is queued stays, and receivers can still take it. A sender that
    already waits for room still gets in. @code{inbox-closed} fires when all
    of that is received.

    A second close does nothing.
    """))

(:doc inbox-fail!
  (summary "Closes the inbox as inbox-close! does, and inbox-closed then gives (Some e).")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (arg e "The error that a receiver gets from @code{inbox-closed}.")
  (see inbox-close! inbox-closed)
  (reference """
    The error is kept beside the queue, not in it. Thus no
    @code{WhenFull} policy can drop the item that tells what went wrong.

    The first error is kept. An error that comes after
    @code{inbox-closed} has fired has no effect.
    """))

(:doc inbox-abort!
  (summary "Closes the inbox and drops everything queued.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (see inbox-close! inbox-closed)
  (reference """
    A hard stop. The queued items are dropped. The task of each queued
    call is cancelled, and each @code{inbox-send} task that waits for room
    is cancelled. A waiting @code{inbox-send-evt} never commits, so its
    @code{choose} stays open for its other branches.

    Then @code{inbox-closed} fires with @code{None}, or with the error of
    an earlier @code{inbox-fail!}.

    The dropped items are not added to @code{inbox-dropped-count}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; What was lost, and subscribing to an IObservable
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc inbox-dropped-count
  (summary "How many items the WhenFull policy has dropped from this inbox.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (returns "The number of items that were refused or removed because the inbox was full.")
  (see WhenFull inbox-post!)
  (reference """
    This lets a program report its losses, for example "dropped 37
    progress updates". Items dropped by @code{inbox-abort!} are not
    counted: that is the program that stops, not an inbox that overflows.
    """))

(:doc inbox-observer
  (summary "The inbox as an IObserver, to subscribe it to an IObservable.")
  (arg ib "The inbox.")
  (returns "An observer whose @code{OnNext} posts, whose @code{OnError} fails the inbox, and whose @code{OnCompleted} closes it.")
  (see make-observer inbox-post! inbox-fail! inbox-close!)
  (example """
    (.Subscribe observable (inbox-observer ib))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{OnNext} uses @code{inbox-post!}, so the @code{WhenFull} policy of
    the inbox applies, and an item that is refused is lost.
    """))

(:doc make-observer
  (summary "An IObserver made of three functions.")
  (arg on-next "Called with each item.")
  (arg on-error "Called with the error that ends the stream.")
  (arg on-completed "Called when the stream ends without an error.")
  (returns "An observer that calls the three functions.")
  (see inbox-observer)
  (reference """
    To subscribe to an @code{IObservable}, you need an object that
    implements @code{IObserver}. Bjolang cannot write such an object, and
    base .NET has no @code{Subscribe(Action<T>)}.

    The three functions run on the thread of the observable. Thus they
    obey the rule for every callback from .NET: post to an inbox, close it
    or fail it, and do nothing else.
    """))

(:doc IObserver
  (summary "The .NET interface System.IObserver, which an IObservable pushes items to.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the items.")
  (see make-observer inbox-observer))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Request and reply
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc inbox-call
  (summary "Posts a request as a Call, and returns the task that its caller waits on. Safe from any thread.")
  (arg ib "The request/reply inbox.")
  (arg request "What the caller asks.")
  (returns "A task that completes when a fiber answers with @code{call-reply!} or @code{call-fail!}.")
  (see inbox-call/token inbox-try-call call-request call-reply!)
  (reference """
    The call is posted as @code{inbox-post!} posts, and never waits. When
    the inbox is full, the @code{WhenFull} policy decides: a call that is
    refused or removed faults its task with a
    @code{Bjoml.InboxFullException}. On a closed inbox, the task is
    cancelled. @code{inbox-abort!} cancels the task of a call that is still
    queued.

    A caller that does not await the task cannot see a fault. Such a
    caller uses @code{inbox-try-call}.
    """))

(:doc inbox-call/token
  (summary "The same as inbox-call, with a cancellation token of the caller.")
  (arg ib "The request/reply inbox.")
  (arg request "What the caller asks.")
  (arg token "The caller's token, for example Kestrel's @code{RequestAborted}.")
  (returns "A task that completes when a fiber answers with @code{call-reply!} or @code{call-fail!}.")
  (see inbox-call call-abandoned inbox-try-call/token)
  (reference """
    What the token does depends on where the call is:

    @itemlist{
    @item{@strong{Still queued.} The call is removed and the task is
    cancelled. No fiber sees a call that nobody waits for.}
    @item{@strong{Already taken by a fiber.} The task is @strong{not}
    completed. Only @code{call-reply!} and @code{call-fail!} complete it.
    @code{call-abandoned} fires instead, and the receiver decides what to
    do.}
    }

    The second rule keeps an HTTP handler from writing into a context that
    is already used again for the next request.
    """))

(:doc inbox-try-call
  (summary "The same as inbox-call, but a refusal is None and not a faulted task.")
  (arg ib "The request/reply inbox.")
  (arg request "What the caller asks.")
  (returns "@code{(Some task)} when the call went in. @code{None} when the inbox was closed, or full under @code{DropNewest}, and nothing was posted.")
  (see inbox-call inbox-try-call/token inbox-post!)
  (reference """
    This is the @code{#f} of @code{inbox-post!}, for a call. A faulted task
    is the wrong answer for a .NET callback that returns its task directly
    and never awaits it, for example an HTTP middleware. That callback
    cannot see the fault, and the exception goes to the handler of the
    framework for unhandled exceptions. A web server then answers 500 where
    it should answer 503: it tells the client to stop, not to come back
    later.

    Under @code{DropOldest} the answer is @code{(Some ...)}: this call went
    in, and the task of an @strong{older} call faults. The @code{#t} of
    @code{inbox-post!} has the same meaning.
    """))

(:doc inbox-try-call/token
  (summary "The same as inbox-try-call, with a cancellation token of the caller.")
  (arg ib "The request/reply inbox.")
  (arg request "What the caller asks.")
  (arg token "The caller's token. It works as for @code{inbox-call/token}.")
  (returns "@code{(Some task)} when the call went in. @code{None} when the inbox was closed, or full under @code{DropNewest}, and nothing was posted.")
  (see inbox-try-call inbox-call/token call-abandoned)
  (example """
    (match (inbox-try-call/token requests ctx (request-aborted ctx))
      ((Some answering) (cast Task answering))
      (None (set-status! (response-of ctx) 503) completed-task))
    """))

(:doc call-request
  (summary "What the caller of a Call asked.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "The request.")
  (see call-reply! call-fail!))

(:doc call-reply!
  (summary "Answers a call with a value. The first answer wins.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (arg value "The answer for the caller.")
  (returns "@code{#t} when this answer completed the task. @code{#f} when the call was already answered or cancelled; then nothing happens.")
  (see call-fail! call-abandoned)
  (reference """
    Because a later answer does nothing, a timeout and a handler can race
    to answer the same call safely.

    The caller's continuation does not run on the thread of the fiber that
    answers.
    """))

(:doc call-fail!
  (summary "Answers a call with an exception. The first answer wins.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (arg e "The exception that the caller's task faults with.")
  (returns "@code{#t} when this answer completed the task. @code{#f} when the call was already answered or cancelled; then nothing happens.")
  (see call-reply!))

(:doc call-abandoned
  (summary "The event that fires when the caller's token fires after a fiber took the call.")
  (arg c "The call.")
  (returns "An event that fires once, and then commits at once on every @code{sync}.")
  (see inbox-call/token inbox-try-call/token call-reply!)
  (example """
    (sync (choose (wrap (work-evt (call-request c))
                        (fun (v) (ignore (call-reply! c v))))
                  (wrap (call-abandoned c)
                        (fun (_) (ignore (call-fail! c (System.OperationCanceledException.)))))))
    """)
  (reference """
    It never fires for a call made without a token. It never fires for a
    call that was cancelled while it was still queued, because no fiber
    had that call.

    The task of the call is not completed when the token fires. The fiber
    that has the call still answers it.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The token an inbox-call/token is given
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc CancellationToken
  (summary "The .NET System.Threading.CancellationToken, as a caller of inbox-call/token or inbox-send/token gives it.")
  (see inbox-call/token inbox-send/token inbox-try-call/token))
